      "writtenOn": "2021-03-17T20:31:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, should a developer need to understand the difference between \"KeyStore\" and \"KeyMint\"?  It almost sounds like KeyMint is an implementation detail behind the scenes.\n\nAt a distance I\u0027m wondering if this should be defined as:\n\n    public static final String FEATURE_KEYSTORE \u003d \"android.hardware.keystore\";",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2183,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2183,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "b4c74c256ba3736f7b267ef411b9a5788cf11eae",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "edc92b5a_dfce1135",
        "filename": "core/java/android/content/pm/PackageManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 2183,
      "author": {
        "id": 1076971
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-18T13:29:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorta. Kinda. It\u0027s complicated. It\u0027s a good question :-)\n\nFirst, if you care about HW-backed Android KeyStore you probably have an application with a server backend and an Android frontend (and likely other mobile frontends and a website). In which case you probably care about things like attestation and here\u0027s where the nice layered \"Android KeyStore is the abstraction, pay no attention to KeyMint behind the curtain\" picture starts to fall apart... I mean, to deal with attestation you\u0027ll need to look at https://source.android.com/security/keystore/attestation which mentions both KeyMaster (and in the future will also mention KeyMint). In particular you likely need to have explicit support for various KeyMaster / KeyMint versions in your server backend.\n\nSo I really don\u0027t see how it\u0027s helpful to hide this implementation detail from the developer, I think in fact it might just add extra confusion. The alternative is that you develop a parallel world with KeyStore versions which map 1:1 to KeyMaster / KeyMint versions (and do things like mirror the stuff from source.android.com on developer.android.com). But that\u0027s just lots of extra busy-work just to avoid using the words KeyMaster and KeyMint.\n\nI guess maybe we could call it FEATURE_KEYSTORE instead of FEATURE_KEYMINT but point out in the JavaDoc that this really is backed by KeyMint and point them to the source.android.com page? Shawn is back on Monday, I kinda want him to weigh in as well.",
      "parentUuid": "b625c29b_17eac9f2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2183,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2183,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "b4c74c256ba3736f7b267ef411b9a5788cf11eae",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1b1714b9_5d17b977",
        "filename": "core/java/android/content/pm/PackageManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 2183,
      "author": {
        "id": 1043663
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-22T13:15:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think Keymaster / KeyMint is an implementation detail that should continue to be hidden from the developer. What we do already have is a distinction between Keystore and Keystore\u0027s hardware support; not all Keystore keys or feature of keys are hardware-backed.  I think something like \"FEATURE_KEYSTORE_HARDWARE\" would use this existing distinction rather than introducing a new concept. Then the definitions of the various levels would express which keystore features are implemented in hardware.",
      "parentUuid": "edc92b5a_dfce1135",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2183,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2183,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "b4c74c256ba3736f7b267ef411b9a5788cf11eae",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d2df097f_348cdb29",
        "filename": "core/java/android/content/pm/PackageManager.java",
